From a983969d0e4169c5bbdbea43f27e6cd37b361f33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Xavier Geerinck <xavier.geerinck@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 16:52:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 20/27] fix(sandbox): never re-run a command cut short by a
 rebalance

On a rebalance the disseminator cancels the in-flight placement claims
of the moved actor types once the drain timeout passes (2 s when the
app sets none). That cancelled the sandbox driver call, the driver
killed the command, and the actor router, which retries a failed call
whose claim context ended, ran the exec or session send again: on the
same VM, or on a fresh one on the actor's new host.

A driver call that runs a command (exec, session start, session send)
now never comes back to the router as retryable:

- If its context ended while it ran, the target answers a new
  "interrupted" error: Aborted with ErrorInfo reason
  DAPR_SANDBOX_INTERRUPTED. Aborted is not a code that a caller's
  router retries across sidecars either.
- Every error after such a driver call is wrapped in
  backoff.Permanent, which the router's retry loop already honours, so
  no change to the shared router or lock code is needed.

The API maps the interrupted error to ERR_SANDBOX_EXEC (500) with the
message "sandbox call interrupted by actor rebalancing: the command may
have partly run". When the caller's own context ended instead, it gets
that context's error, as before. A call interrupted before it reached
the driver (waiting for its turn, or acquiring) is still retried: no
command ran.

The test drives the real router with a fake placement and cancels the
claim mid-exec: one lookup, one driver call, the interrupted error.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
